      "topic": "Central Moments Consistency",
      "questionText": "Examine whether the following results of a piece of computation for obtaining the second central moment are consistent or not: N = 120, sum(fX) = -125, sum(fX^2) = 128.",
      "tables": [],
      "answer": {
        "status": "complete",
        "steps": [
          "The second central moment $\\mu_2$ represents variance, which must always be non-negative ($\\mu_2 \\ge 0$).",
          "Raw moments: $\\mu'_1 = \\frac{\\sum fX}{N} = \\frac{-125}{120} = -1.0417$.",
          "$\\mu'_2 = \\frac{\\sum fX^2}{N} = \\frac{128}{120} = 1.0667$.",
          "$\\mu_2 = \\mu'_2 - (\\mu'_1)^2 = 1.0667 - (-1.0417)^2 = 1.0667 - 1.0851 = -0.0184$.",
          "Since $\\mu_2 = -0.0184 < 0$, variance cannot be negative. Hence, the results are INCONSISTENT."
        ],
        "finalAnswer": "Inconsistent (\\mu_2 = -0.0184 < 0)",
        "tableValuesUsed": "None",
        "sourceLink": "https://chatgpt.com/share/6af4959d-3f40-83ee-a7bc-7562286c5b1b"
      },
