      "questionText": "A political candidate claims that at least 60% of the population supports her. A Poll is conducted where 1500 people are surveyed, and 870 of them support her. Test the candidate's claim at 1% significance level.",
      "tables": [],
      "answer": {
        "status": "complete",
        "steps": [
          "Null hypothesis $H_0: P \\ge 0.60$ vs Alternative $H_1: P < 0.60$ (One-tailed test).",
          "Sample proportion $p = \\frac{870}{1500} = 0.58$.",
          "Standard Error $SE = \\sqrt{\\frac{P(1-P)}{n}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{0.60 \\times 0.40}{1500}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{0.24}{1500}} = \\sqrt{0.00016} = 0.01265$.",
          "Test statistic $Z = \\frac{p - P}{SE} = \\frac{0.58 - 0.60}{0.01265} = \\frac{-0.02}{0.01265} = -1.58$.",
          "Critical value at $\\alpha = 0.01$ for left-tailed test is $-2.33$.",
          "Since $Z_{calc} = -1.58 > -2.33$, it does not fall in the critical region. We fail to reject $H_0$ at 1% significance level. The candidate's claim is supported."
        ],
        "finalAnswer": "Fail to reject H0 (Z = -1.58 > -2.33; claim accepted at 1% level)",
        "tableValuesUsed": "Z_critical = -2.33 at 1% one-tailed",
